1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:03,873
A product relaunch is not a single switch. Supply, assortment,

2
00:00:03,873 --> 00:00:07,836
customer codes, and approvals must move together, often across different teams.

3
00:00:08,636 --> 00:00:11,606
When those handoffs are fragmented, the next team can miss what

4
00:00:11,606 --> 00:00:14,576
changed. The portal brings the linked work into a traceable view.

5
00:00:15,886 --> 00:00:18,262
Network Planning follows a material through its lifecycle.

6
00:00:18,262 --> 00:00:20,341
Teams can inspect the predecessor and successor,

7
00:00:20,341 --> 00:00:22,716
the change type, and the intended network dates.

8
00:00:23,516 --> 00:00:26,474
Sourcing and distribution views show which geographies, depots, and channels

9
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:29,728
are in scope. Readiness is checked before a request moves onward.

10
00:00:30,766 --> 00:00:33,132
A service request carries the proposed change into

11
00:00:33,132 --> 00:00:35,793
review. Status and approval keep the decision explicit, so

12
00:00:35,793 --> 00:00:38,158
teams can distinguish planned work from accepted work.

13
00:00:38,958 --> 00:00:41,098
Paid Bible connects basepacks and materials to the

14
00:00:41,098 --> 00:00:42,971
places they must be sold. Teams see

15
00:00:42,971 --> 00:00:45,111
the geography and channel combinations that need attention.

16
00:00:46,208 --> 00:00:48,604
Readiness and request review expose the remaining work.

17
00:00:48,604 --> 00:00:51,300
That helps a reviewer see what is configured, what

18
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:53,696
is pending, and what still needs a decision.

19
00:00:54,496 --> 00:00:58,995
CCR then focuses on customer-code continuity. Predecessor and successor CBUs,

20
00:00:58,995 --> 00:01:03,494
chain-specific mappings, and SAP-update status make the commercial handoff reviewable.

21
00:01:04,294 --> 00:01:07,817
At MT Listing, Export ATP Payload downloads JSON built

22
00:01:07,817 --> 00:01:10,949
from output records. Its last-run timestamp is stored

23
00:01:10,949 --> 00:01:14,472
in this browser. It does not fetch live ATP.

24
00:01:15,272 --> 00:01:18,207
The detail view demonstrates ATP criteria by customer

25
00:01:18,207 --> 00:01:21,509
code, CBU, depot, and batch. It checks stock age

26
00:01:21,509 --> 00:01:24,444
against valid days and whether pricing is valid.

27
00:01:25,244 --> 00:01:28,018
In the guided exception demo, customer classification and

28
00:01:28,018 --> 00:01:31,139
the change-type ruleset come first. Missing rules, a new

29
00:01:31,139 --> 00:01:33,913
CIC requirement, or rundown can stop a proposal.

30
00:01:34,713 --> 00:01:37,403
The demo compares usable successor ATP with eligible

31
00:01:37,403 --> 00:01:40,092
alternatives at the depot. National non-listing cases also

32
00:01:40,092 --> 00:01:42,782
check days of holding and the CLOT gate.

33
00:01:43,582 --> 00:01:46,355
The result can retain, switch, defer, or escalate

34
00:01:46,355 --> 00:01:49,129
a mapping. The demo presents a before-and-after proposal

35
00:01:49,129 --> 00:01:51,902
for human review; it never executes the change.

36
00:01:52,702 --> 00:01:55,260
Admin Center supports the reference data and access

37
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:57,498
behind these workflows. Clear ownership of those

38
00:01:57,498 --> 00:02:00,055
foundations helps teams interpret the same change consistently.

39
00:02:00,855 --> 00:02:04,483
Together, these views let teams follow a relaunch from network plan

40
00:02:04,483 --> 00:02:08,112
to assortment and customer-code mapping, while keeping exceptions and approvals visible.

41
00:02:08,912 --> 00:02:11,285
The aim is straightforward: fewer ambiguous handoffs, a

42
00:02:11,285 --> 00:02:13,659
clearer record of what is ready, and better

43
00:02:13,659 --> 00:02:16,032
context for the people making the next decision.
